<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's Front for Democracy and Justice</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as PFDJ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People's Front for Democracy and Justice is the ruling and only legal party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, formed in February 1994 from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">Eritrean People's Liberation Front</a>, which had won the war of independence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Led by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaias-afwerki">Isaias Afwerki</a>, it has governed without national elections since independence, under a constitution ratified in 1997 but never implemented.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peoples-front-for-democracy-and-justice.jpg" alt="People's Front for Democracy and Justice" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Permjak · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_EPLF.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> as an autonomous unit in 1952. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-movement">Eritrean Liberation Movement</a> was founded in 1958 and succeeded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-front">Eritrean Liberation Front</a> (ELF) in 1961, which grew after Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> abolished Eritrea's autonomy and annexed it as a province in 1962. Through the 1960s and 1970s the ELF waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war against the Ethiopian government, and in 1970 a faction broke away to form the Marxist-Leninist Eritrean People's Liberation Front (EPLF).</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">EPLF</a> won control of much of the countryside and built administrations in the areas it held. Fighting among the EPLF, the ELF and other rebel groups from 1981 checked its advance, but it emerged as the principal Eritrean guerrilla group. As Soviet support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s socialist government collapsed in the late 1980s, it allied with guerrillas in Tigray and elsewhere in Ethiopia. After a referendum supervised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in April 1993, it declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> independent the following month.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">EPLF</a>'s third congress delegates voted to turn the 95,000-member organisation into a mass political movement, with a transitional mandate to draw the population into politics and prepare the country for constitutional democracy. The new party set up a transitional National Assembly of 150 members, which chose its secretary-general, the former EPLF leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaias-afwerki">Isaias Afwerki</a>, as president. In 1997 the assembly adopted a constitution for a multi-party system. After independence a committee of former senior fighters reviewed the laws inherited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> to adapt them to the new state and to the EPLF's values.</p><p>An early attempt to shape the country's values failed because the former fighters were a minority, but the border war of 1998 to 2000 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> gave the party a second chance to impose its ideology. On 12 November 2021 the Office of Foreign Assets Control of the U.S. Department of the Treasury placed the party on its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> list for activities contributing to the crisis in northern Ethiopia; the sanctions were lifted on 19 September 2026.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party describes itself as left-wing and nationalist but holds itself open to nationalists of any political affiliation. Observers debate whether it is a true party or a broad governing association in transition, and some see it as an amorphous nationalist umbrella.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “People's Front for Democracy and Justice” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The EPLF became the <b>People's Front for Democracy and Justice</b>, the only legal party.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-eritrea">State of Eritrea</a></li></ul></section>
